WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.078
Training a recurrent network applies backpropagation to its unrolled computational graph.

00:00:06.078 --> 00:00:11.142
Each hidden state influences the output at its own step and also later states.

00:00:11.142 --> 00:00:20.620
Therefore its sensitivity combines the direct contribution from its local prediction loss with the contribution arriving backward from the future.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:27.372
Multiply this combined sensitivity by the tanh derivative to obtain the local preactivation sensitivity.

00:00:27.372 --> 00:00:33.020
This is the same multiply-along-paths and add-at-branches rule introduced in Lecture 9.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:41.187
For each time step, form an outer product with the previous hidden state to contribute to the recurrent-weight gradient.

00:00:41.187 --> 00:00:45.812
Form another with the input to contribute to the input-weight gradient.

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:50.242
Accumulate those contributions over all uses of the shared matrices.

00:00:50.242 --> 00:00:55.258
The output-weight gradient similarly sums contributions from the step losses.

00:00:55.258 --> 00:01:00.600
If the objective is an average over time, include that normalization consistently.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:07.570
The training lab checks these derivatives against finite differences before using them in gradient descent.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:17.367
Full backpropagation through time retains or reconstructs the intermediate values across the sequence, which can be expensive for long inputs.

00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:20.028
Truncated BPTT processes shorter chunks.

00:01:20.028 --> 00:01:26.747
A hidden-state value may be carried into the next chunk while its older gradient history is detached.

00:01:26.747 --> 00:01:33.998
This preserves forward information but restricts which earlier computations receive credit from a later loss.

00:01:33.998 --> 00:01:38.322
Resetting state and detaching its graph are different operations.

00:01:38.322 --> 00:01:45.640
The lecture's diagrams emphasize this distinction by carrying states forward while limiting the backward span.

00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:52.040
A recurrent gradient can contain a long product of local Jacobians.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:55.052
Products can shrink or grow rapidly.

00:01:55.052 --> 00:02:01.077
For intuition, 10 repeated scalar factors of 0.5 produce about 0.000977,

00:02:01.077 --> 00:02:04.173
whereas 10 factors of 2 produce 1024.

00:02:04.173 --> 00:02:11.956
Real networks involve matrices and activation derivatives, so their behavior is more complex.

00:02:11.956 --> 00:02:15.386
This calculation is only an illustration.

00:02:15.386 --> 00:02:21.913
Real gradient behavior depends on all the matrices and activation derivatives.

00:02:22.263 --> 00:02:25.639
Exploding gradients can cause unstable updates.

00:02:25.639 --> 00:02:34.186
Global norm clipping rescales a gradient when its norm exceeds a chosen threshold, leaving smaller gradients unchanged.

00:02:34.186 --> 00:02:38.711
For example, a gradient vector with entries 3 and 4 has norm 5.

00:02:38.711 --> 00:02:41.871
A threshold of 2 rescales it to 1.2 and 1.6.

00:02:41.871 --> 00:02:47.257
Coordinate-wise clipping is another convention and gives different results.

00:02:47.257 --> 00:02:49.053
State which rule is used.

00:02:49.053 --> 00:02:54.583
Our training lab exposes the norm threshold and applies one global rescaling.

00:02:54.933 --> 00:02:59.959
Vanishing gradients make distant earlier events receive very little training signal.

00:02:59.959 --> 00:03:04.924
Capping large gradients does not restore a derivative that has already become tiny.

00:03:04.924 --> 00:03:08.633
The slide's combined discussion should therefore be qualified:

00:03:08.633 --> 00:03:10.787
clipping mainly mitigates explosion.

00:03:10.787 --> 00:03:15.812
Gating and additive connections can improve the training signal over long distances,

00:03:15.812 --> 00:03:18.205
but no method guarantees perfect recall.

00:03:18.205 --> 00:03:21.794
RNNs also retain a sequential state dependency at inference.

00:03:21.794 --> 00:03:24.965
These limitations motivate both gated recurrent cells

00:03:24.965 --> 00:03:28.853
and the attention architecture we will examine in the final part.

00:03:29.633 --> 00:03:35.412
An LSTM maintains a cell state and a hidden state, rather than only one hidden vector.

00:03:35.412 --> 00:03:45.692
Concatenate the previous hidden state and current input, then apply learned affine projections to compute input, forget and output gates through sigmoid.

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:49.858
A separate projection and tanh compute candidate cell content.

00:03:49.858 --> 00:03:55.031
These operations act on vectors; products in the cell update are elementwise.

00:03:55.031 --> 00:04:02.153
The slides sometimes call the candidate g a gate, but unlike the sigmoid gates its values can be negative.

00:04:02.503 --> 00:04:07.239
The new cell state combines retained old content and newly written content.

00:04:07.239 --> 00:04:12.606
Multiply the old cell by the forget gate, and add the input gate times the candidate.

00:04:12.606 --> 00:04:17.405
The output gate multiplies tanh of the new cell to produce the hidden state.

00:04:17.405 --> 00:04:25.297
A forget gate near 1 can preserve the direct cell path more readily than repeated multiplication through a tanh state update.

00:04:25.297 --> 00:04:34.263
The direct path derivative includes the forget factor, while the complete derivative also includes dependencies through the gate computations.

00:04:34.613 --> 00:04:44.602
Calculate one component with old cell 1, forget gate 0.8, input gate 0.25, candidate 0.4 and output gate 0.5.

00:04:44.602 --> 00:04:52.117
The retained content is 0.8 and the written content is 0.1, giving cell state 0.9.

00:04:52.117 --> 00:04:57.982
The hidden state is half of tanh of 0.9, approximately 0.358149.

00:04:57.982 --> 00:05:01.007
Try the interactive gate sliders.

00:05:01.007 --> 00:05:13.653
The result illustrates information flow; the gates in a trained LSTM are computed from learned projections, not manually chosen constants.

00:05:14.433 --> 00:05:18.530
A GRU uses gating without a separate LSTM-style cell vector.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:28.841
An update gate interpolates between the old state and a candidate, while a reset gate controls how the previous state enters the candidate calculation.

00:05:28.841 --> 00:05:34.576
We use the convention where update value z means the fraction of old state retained.

00:05:34.576 --> 00:05:42.292
Some presentations reverse this convention, and implementations also differ in where reset multiplication occurs.

00:05:42.292 --> 00:05:49.393
Always read the equations rather than transferring a gate's informal name between incompatible formulas.

00:05:49.743 --> 00:05:57.469
For our explicit convention, an old state of 0.6, retention gate 0.75 and candidate -0.2 gives 0.4.

00:05:57.469 --> 00:06:03.323
Most of the old state is retained, with one quarter of the candidate added.

00:06:03.323 --> 00:06:15.731
A GRU commonly uses three affine projections compared with four for a standard LSTM, so it usually has fewer parameters at the same widths and bias convention.

00:06:15.731 --> 00:06:23.223
It is still a trainable recurrent architecture, not a universal fix for every long-context task.

00:06:23.573 --> 00:06:26.773
The optional slides also stack recurrent layers.

00:06:26.773 --> 00:06:35.773
At each time step, the hidden output of a lower layer becomes input to the next layer; within each layer, state also flows across time.

00:06:35.773 --> 00:06:37.707
Keep these two axes separate.

00:06:37.707 --> 00:06:46.707
A bidirectional extension uses both temporal directions and cannot use unrestricted future context in a strictly causal streaming task.

00:06:46.707 --> 00:06:54.507
Greater depth does not guarantee improvement, and example depth counts in an older deck are not architectural limits.

00:06:54.507 --> 00:06:59.173
Evaluate the task, data, optimization and computational cost together.

00:06:59.967 --> 00:07:05.936
Pause and predict an idealized LSTM component with forget gate 1 and input gate 0.

00:07:05.936 --> 00:07:07.901
What is its new cell state?

00:07:07.901 --> 00:07:11.104
Must the hidden state equal that cell state?

00:07:11.104 --> 00:07:19.330
Then explain whether a forget gate near 1 guarantees that every gradient in a full recurrent model remains large.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:27.847
Distinguish the direct additive cell path from all the other dependencies in the computation graph before you answer.

00:07:28.397 --> 00:07:35.107
The new cell equals the old cell because it is entirely retained and no candidate content is written.

00:07:35.107 --> 00:07:41.351
The hidden state still applies tanh and an output gate, so it generally differs from the cell.

00:07:41.351 --> 00:07:49.656
Gates near 1 can improve a particular route for information and gradients, but do not guarantee healthy gradients everywhere.

00:07:49.656 --> 00:07:58.957
Your exercise is to compute both states for several slider settings, then derive how the cell changes when only its input gate is perturbed.

00:07:59.307 --> 00:08:01.810
Three conclusions connect this part.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:06.190
BPTT accumulates gradients through shared parameters over time.

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:13.004
Gradient clipping and gated paths address different failure mechanisms and should not be confused.

00:08:13.004 --> 00:08:19.748
LSTM and GRU gates control information flow, while stacked models add a separate depth dimension.

00:08:19.748 --> 00:08:27.604
Next, attention will compare sequence positions directly rather than relying only on one recurrent state pathway.

00:08:27.604 --> 00:08:34.627
We will calculate its weights, apply causal masking and place it inside a complete Transformer block.

